  "headline": "Gold and copper bid again with the dollar slipping; the shape holds, I still wait for $3,700",
  "body": [
    "Second day of the same shape, and it is the one my thesis needs: gold up 1.5% and copper up 2.7% while the dollar slipped, with the Treasury market and stocks quoting their previous close rather than trading. Hard assets rising, the dollar failing to hold a high at 93% of its range, and fear easing rather than spiking is not a panic bid. It is the slow tide I keep describing, and I lean with it, but half my evidence is stale today because the bond market did not price it.",
    "Yesterday I said a one-day bid was not enough and set a bar of $3,700 held through the week. Gold is at $3,655, up 3.4% on the week, so the bid has legs but has not cleared the bar, and I was right not to add. What I did not expect was copper leading gold on the day; that is a growth signal, not a fear signal, and I note it rather than explain it away.",
    "The tells line up better than they have in a month. The dollar index eased on a day when the VIX fell to 26.8, so this is not safe-haven dollar buying unwinding, it is the dollar being sold while hard assets are bought. USD/CAD fell 0.4% and USD/INR fell 0.6%, so producers and importers were both paid against the dollar; with Brent at $92 and down 17% on the month, there is still no oil shock reaching the rupee, which is why the importer leg of the thesis stays dormant.",
    "Bitcoin lagged gold today, up 1% against 1.5%, reversing yesterday, and one bitcoin now buys 16.8 ounces, down 15% on the month. That says gold is being bought as money and bitcoin is still trading as a risk asset at 8% of its range, which is the wrong configuration to own it in. The curve, last traded, still has the 30-year minus 5-year spread at 58 basis points, up 10 on the month; the long end was not rallying with the front before the close, and that is the term premium I want to see built. Nothing in today's headlines has a channel to these markets, so I draw on none.",
    "I hold the gold I have, keep the 10-year at 4.85%, keep the dollar as a hedge for one more session, and stay out of bitcoin and the 30-year at 5.24%. Copper up on the day is not enough to take me in after a 15% monthly drop. I add to gold on a weekly close above $3,700 with the VIX above 25; gold below $3,450 with the dollar at a new high ends it. When Treasuries reopen I want the 30-year to lag any front-end rally; if the long end leads the rally instead, this is a plain recession and I shift to the 30-year. MOVE at 97.5, 71% up its range, is the number that tells me whether the bond market agrees."
  ],
